# Compatibility register and native fallback

Every compatibility claim needs a scope, version context, evidence type and usable fallback. Theme variables, exact presentation adapters, custom components and documentation specimens are different kinds of support. Matching colours alone do not prove a native task is supported.

## Scope and native ownership

Home Assistant supports theme variables and extra frontend modules, while custom cards have a separate API. Private native structures are not public compatibility promises. The register below describes the current repository’s explicit targets and separates inspected source from historical executed evidence. [Official Home Assistant source](https://www.home-assistant.io/integrations/frontend/), reviewed 11 November 2026.

This page completes the design specification for **HA-GAP-030**. Its examples are synthetic documentation, not a claim that a new native feature or adapter has been installed. The application integration must meet the task-specific acceptance below.

## States and content decisions

| Source condition | Required presentation | Boundary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Token-only coverage | State which public variables the component consumes. | Do not promise access to a closed or private root. |
| Exact adapter supported | Name host, owned changes, evidence and cleanup. | Do not extend an exact selector to all similar cards. |
| Target changed / absent | Preserve native interface and surface-specific fallback. | Do not delete controls or throw into the host lifecycle. |
| Default theme / recovery | Remove owned presentation and preserve current native values. | Do not restore stale user data or hide native diagnostics. |
| Reference example | Label synthetic behaviour and integration requirements. | A working local form is not a connected Home Assistant component. |

## Integration contract

1. For every new adapter, record repository version/commit, Core/frontend and custom-card versions, exact host/engine, discovery boundary, owned styles/attributes and restoration rules.
2. Separate source review, automated fixture execution, manual browser interaction and live installation evidence. An inspected development commit is not the installed frontend.
3. Use bounded discovery and avoid browser-prototype patches. Repeated imports, node replacement, navigation and changing theme must not accumulate observers, listeners or duplicate controls.
4. On removal, restore only values still owned by the adapter. Preserve later native updates and the latest source data; never write .storage or dashboard configuration to repair a presentation failure.

## Accessible interaction

- Fallback must retain accessible native controls, names, focus and diagnostics. A source-shape fixture pass does not cover a newly changed private structure.
- Check both light/dark and forced colours for owned elements, then assess the actual surrounding native component.
- Document unsupported interactions and closed roots plainly. Do not make a native component appear available while masking its rejection or missing capability.

## Failure and recovery

- If Profile insertion is unavailable, retain the native theme dropdown and documented standalone settings card.
- If an Energy renderer is absent, disposed or unsupported, remove owned plot/table presentation and retain the native route; do not synthesize readings.
- If a custom card changes structure, remove the exact compatibility sheet rather than broadening selectors to unrelated descendants.

## Current implementation register

| Surface and exact boundary | Coverage and ownership | Evidence and fallback |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Native theme variables | Generated civica themes; component consumes supported tokens | Palette/unit checks; unsupported private variables retain native behaviour |
| `ha-pick-theme-row` | Companion adds palette/tone controls; native theme event/persistence remain authoritative | Theme-settings fixture; native dropdown and standalone `custom:civica-theme-settings` fallback |
| `ha-sidebar` | Supplied identity, icon contrast and numeric MOJ-derived attention badge | Theme/badge fixtures; restore owned attributes and disconnect observer when inactive |
| `hass-subpage`, `ha-top-app-bar-fixed` | Scoped toolbar icon/focus contrast | Nested native-shaped fixture; no replacement of toolbar actions |
| `pa-appliance-card`, `home-next-collection-card`, `pa-gas-safety-card` | Exact adopted presentation sheets only | Legacy-card fixture; card data, permissions, actions and native markup remain authoritative |
| `ha-panel-energy` and seven supported Cartesian hosts | Existing ECharts presentation, stable colours, signed Power view and read-only values table | Real-renderer/source-shape fixtures; restore owned options and retain native data when unsupported |
| `custom:civica-action-links` | Maintained real local anchors and existing fragment destinations | Action-link fixture; no device commands or invented destinations |
| `custom:civica-house-lighting-card` | Maintained light summary/control card and bounded native more-info scene presentation | Lighting fixture; supported service capabilities, reserved ownership and native dialog lifecycle remain explicit |
| History, native Energy distribution, Map, authentication, arbitrary third-party cards | No Energy/private-brand adapter claim for these families | Native behaviour and supported public tokens; separate task-specific live acceptance |

The exact Energy hosts are `hui-power-sources-graph-card`, `hui-energy-usage-graph-card`, `hui-energy-solar-graph-card`, `hui-energy-gas-graph-card`, `hui-energy-water-graph-card`, `hui-energy-devices-graph-card` and `hui-energy-devices-detail-graph-card`. The native Energy distribution diagram is not an ONS Sankey. History and non-ECharts/custom renderers do not gain the Energy values table through this adapter.

Read [the installation compatibility contract](../get-started/compatibility.html), [chart specification](../components/charts.html), [card presentation](../components/cards.html) and [native task matrix](native-task-accessibility.html) alongside the register. Repository implementation evidence is in [the companion](https://github.com/nathcymru/HA-Theme-gov.uk/blob/main/ui/src/civica-ui.ts), [Energy adapter](https://github.com/nathcymru/HA-Theme-gov.uk/blob/main/ui/src/energy-charts.ts) and [lighting card](https://github.com/nathcymru/HA-Theme-gov.uk/tree/main/card/src). Mutable source links describe current ownership; record an immutable commit with each executed test.

## Versioned evidence boundary

The Energy source contract explicitly identifies frontend **20261028.5**. The [published fc393e4 verification record](https://github.com/nathcymru/HA-Theme-gov.uk/blob/fc393e49205926b753d1c531358c5e627c9c2af6/VERIFICATION.md) distinguishes historical fixture checks from release-specific live observations. The [published 531-check browser run](https://github.com/nathcymru/HA-Theme-gov.uk/actions/runs/43103696048) covers that documentation/fixture revision; it does not certify this expanded specification or an uninspected installation. Current official documentation was reviewed on 11 November 2026; that source review is a separate evidence type.

A compatibility release record must name the actual Core/frontend/browser and every relevant custom-card version, the tasks/states executed, the theme context, result, artifacts, unresolved limits and rollback preconditions. Until those fields are completed, keep a new installation or changed private host **not tested**.

